---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Hackathon PM Agent | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I designed a verified PRD-first agent workflow that won Best Use of AI and became a catalyst for an evolving system of product-management skills and agents."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--violet">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back">← Back to selected work</a>

        <span class="tag">AI product · Agent interaction · Microsoft hackathon</span>
        <h1>Replacing a requirements maze with a verified agent workflow</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          In a three-day Microsoft hackathon, I turned a repeated wiki, form, routing, and manual
          work-creation process into a skill that starts with a PRD. The agent extracts what it
          can, asks targeted questions, produces a report for confirmation, and keeps downstream
          actions under the product manager's control.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Winner</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Best Use of AI, awarded to our three-person team</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">3 days</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">From product concept to working proof of concept</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Catalyst</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Foundational contribution to evolving PM skills and agents</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <aside class="disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
          <strong>About the evidence</strong>
          <p>This project was completed inside Microsoft. I reconstructed the workflow and interaction with generic content and removed internal requirements, contacts, product details, and work-management data.</p>
        </aside>

        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior Content Designer; concept originator, product driver, and experience owner</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Ownership</dt><dd>Skill, most agent instructions, complete interaction, full experience design, and demo video</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Team</dt><dd>Three people: me, another designer, and a product manager</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Constraint</dt><dd>Three-day official Microsoft hackathon</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Working proof of concept incorporated into an active, evolving PM system</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Best Use of AI, judges' assessment, broad PM sharing, and generic reconstructed artifacts</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Hackathon PM agent case study sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#problem">Workflow gap</a></li>
            <li><a href="#decision">Product decision</a></li>
            <li><a href="#interaction">Agent interaction</a></li>
            <li><a href="#validation">Validation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#evolution">Evolution</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="problem" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The repeated operational problem</h2>
          <p>
            Product managers across teams followed the same manual sequence whenever they needed
            to turn a product idea into assigned work.
          </p>
          <p>
            They searched a wiki containing more than 50 fundamental requirements, completed a
            form to determine which requirements applied, identified the right groups and
            contacts, and then created and assigned work manually.
          </p>
          <p>
            The information existed, but the workflow forced each product manager to repeatedly
            locate, interpret, route, and recreate it. The form did not remove that work. It told
            people where to look for more work.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="role-title">
          <h2 id="role-title">Acting as the product driver</h2>
          <p>
            The idea and product plan were mine. I created the skill, wrote most of the agent
            instructions, designed the complete interaction and experience, and produced the demo
            video used in our presentation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The product manager gave me the domain foundation I needed. They explained how the
            wiki, form, requirements, routing, and contacts worked, created test PRDs, and helped
            with the agent runtime and work-management integration.
          </p>
          <p>
            I was working as the designer and the product driver. I defined the problem, decided
            how the interaction should work, and drove the proof of concept from idea to
            presentation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="decision" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The form was not the experience to preserve</h2>
          <p>
            We considered keeping the wiki as the primary experience, simplifying the form,
            packaging the concept as a standalone application, and pursuing other hackathon ideas.
          </p>
          <p>
            I did not think a shorter form solved the real problem. The product manager already
            had a PRD containing useful context. Asking them to re-enter that information created
            work without improving the result.
          </p>
          <p>
            We used the wiki and form as source material for the agent instead. The skill starts
            with the PRD, infers what it can, and asks only for missing information. This changed
            the interaction from repeated data entry to focused verification.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="workflow-caption">
          <div class="workflow-compare" role="img" aria-label="Before and after PM workflow comparison showing wiki search, form routing, and manual work creation replaced by a PRD-first verified agent workflow">
            <section class="workflow-panel workflow-panel--before">
              <span class="diagram-label">Before</span>
              <h3>Repeated manual process</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Search 50+ fundamentals</li>
                <li>Complete routing form</li>
                <li>Find groups and contacts</li>
                <li>Create and assign work</li>
              </ol>
            </section>
            <div class="workflow-shift" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
            <section class="workflow-panel workflow-panel--after">
              <span class="diagram-label">After</span>
              <h3>Verified agent workflow</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Provide PRD</li>
                <li>Answer targeted questions</li>
                <li>Verify and save report</li>
                <li>Initiate follow-on actions</li>
              </ol>
            </section>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="workflow-caption">
            <strong>Start with existing context.</strong> The PRD supplies the foundation, and the
            agent asks only what is missing before producing a report for confirmation.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="interaction" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Designing the agent interaction</h2>
          <p>
            The agent reads the PRD and identifies available context such as the feature type,
            release stage, schedule, dependencies, related team needs, and likely contacts.
          </p>
          <p>
            It asks targeted follow-up questions where the PRD does not provide enough
            information. The product manager does not need to repeat details the agent already
            found.
          </p>
          <p>The agent then produces a complete draft report containing:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The applicable fundamentals</li>
            <li>The source or rationale for each one</li>
            <li>The responsible contact</li>
            <li>Basic requirement information</li>
            <li>The downstream work the system can help set up</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The product manager can ask for deeper information from the agent's knowledge base before confirming the report.</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="conversation-caption">
          <div class="conversation" aria-label="Generic reconstructed conversation between a product manager and the agent">
            <div class="message message--user"><span>Product manager</span><p>Here is the PRD for a new feature in an upcoming release.</p></div>
            <div class="message message--agent"><span>Agent</span><p>I found the feature type and release stage. Does this experience depend on an existing team capability?</p></div>
            <div class="message message--user"><span>Product manager</span><p>Yes. It uses a shared platform capability.</p></div>
            <div class="report-preview">
              <span>Draft report</span>
              <strong>Applicable fundamental</strong>
              <p>Requirement summary, rationale, responsible contact, and proposed follow-on work.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="conversation-caption">
            <strong>A reconstructed interaction.</strong> The agent combines PRD context with one
            targeted answer, then shows its reasoning and proposed work for review.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="confirmation-title">
          <h2 id="confirmation-title">Separating report approval from action</h2>
          <p>The confirmation model was a key safety decision.</p>
          <p>
            First, the product manager verifies and confirms the complete report. Only then does
            the system save it. After saving, the agent prompts the product manager to initiate
            work setup and other action items separately.
          </p>
          <p>
            Report approval is not blanket permission to perform every downstream action. The
            product manager remains in control of each consequential next step.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="agent-flow-caption">
          <ol class="agent-flow">
            <li><span>1</span><strong>Read PRD</strong><small>Extract available context</small></li>
            <li><span>2</span><strong>Infer</strong><small>Identify fundamentals, dependencies, and contacts</small></li>
            <li><span>3</span><strong>Ask</strong><small>Gather only missing information</small></li>
            <li><span>4</span><strong>Report</strong><small>Show rationale, contacts, and proposed work</small></li>
            <li><span>5</span><strong>Verify</strong><small>PM reviews, corrects, and confirms</small></li>
            <li><span>6</span><strong>Act</strong><small>Initiate follow-on actions individually</small></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="agent-flow-caption">
            <strong>Two layers of control.</strong> The report is approved and saved before the PM
            chooses any downstream work-management action.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="validation" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Validation in three days</h2>
          <p>
            We tested the skill against sample PRDs created by the product manager. The
            three-person team used those examples to check whether the agent extracted the right
            context, asked useful follow-up questions, and produced a report that could guide
            downstream work.
          </p>
          <p>
            The hackathon judges recognized that the concept could eliminate hours of repeated
            wiki search, form completion, contact routing, and manual work creation. That was
            their assessment of the opportunity, not a measured production result.
          </p>
          <p>Our team won Best Use of AI.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="evolution" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>From proof of concept to evolving system</h2>
          <p>The hackathon version was a working proof of concept, not the unchanged final system.</p>
          <p>
            After the event, the skill was shared across the wider product-management organization
            and became an input to a larger system of PM skills and agents. That broader effort
            standardized more of the product-management workflow across the business group and
            continued to evolve through contributions from multiple teams.
          </p>
          <p>
            The proof of concept was a catalyst and foundational contribution during that shift.
            It did not create the later ecosystem alone, but it demonstrated a different way to
            handle the fundamentals and gave teams something concrete to build from.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="evolution-grid" aria-label="Project evolution">
          <div><span>01</span><strong>Proof of concept</strong><small>Three-day working skill and tested interaction</small></div>
          <div><span>02</span><strong>Wider PM sharing</strong><small>A concrete workflow for teams to evaluate and extend</small></div>
          <div><span>03</span><strong>Evolving system</strong><small>Standardized skills and agents shaped by multiple teams</small></div>
        </div>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>
            The key product decision was not adding chat to a form. It was changing where the work
            began, using the PRD as context, asking only what was missing, and preserving
            confirmation before consequential actions.
          </p>
          <p>
            I think the project also showed how quickly a useful system can emerge when the
            interaction model is clear. Three days was enough to prove the concept because we
            focused on one repeated problem, one meaningful source of context, and one safe path
            from understanding to action.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="closing-panel" aria-labelledby="closing-title">
          <span class="tag">Reusable product workflow</span>
          <h2 id="closing-title">From repeated process to reusable skill</h2>
          <p>The proof of concept turned a manual requirements workflow into a verified agent interaction and helped establish a broader direction for how product managers could use skills and agents in their daily work.</p>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <div class="nav-row">
          <a href={`${base}/work/ai-design-repo`} class="btn btn--outline">← Previous: AI design repository</a>
          <a href={`${base}/work/org-ai-initiatives`} class="btn">Next: Org AI initiatives →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .outcome__num { font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 11cqw, 2rem); line-height: 1.1; }
  .outcome__label { max-width: 25ch; font-size: var(--size-sm); color: var(--muted); }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .content-section ul { margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6); }
  .content-section li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }

  .disclosure { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: 0 auto var(--space-12); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; }
  .disclosure strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .disclosure p { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }

  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; line-height: 1.2; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc li { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-coral); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .case-toc li + li { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .case-toc li + li::before { content: ''; width: 2px; height: 16px; margin-right: var(--space-4); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--toc-divider); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n + 1) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.3; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }

  .evidence-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption { width: 100%; margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }

  .workflow-compare { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-3); align-items: stretch; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 20px; background: #fff; }
  .workflow-panel { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .workflow-panel--before { background: var(--portfolio-cream); }
  .workflow-panel:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .workflow-panel h3 { margin-block: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); color: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .workflow-panel ol { margin-left: var(--space-5, 1.25rem); }
  .workflow-panel li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .diagram-label { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .workflow-shift { display: grid; place-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; }

  .conversation { display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); }
  .message, .report-preview { max-width: 78%; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .message:hover, .report-preview:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .message--user { justify-self: end; border-bottom-right-radius: 4px; }
  .message--agent { justify-self: start; border-bottom-left-radius: 4px; }
  .message span, .report-preview > span { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.07em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .message p, .report-preview p { margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .report-preview { max-width: 100%; border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .report-preview strong { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-ink); }

  .agent-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #e1c35f; border-radius: 20px; background: #fff8da; list-style: none; }
  .agent-flow li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 185px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .agent-flow li:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .agent-flow li > span { display: grid; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .agent-flow strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .agent-flow small { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.7rem; line-height: 1.45; }

  .evolution-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evolution-grid div { display: flex; min-height: 165px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .evolution-grid div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .evolution-grid span { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
  .evolution-grid strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); line-height: 1.35; }
  .evolution-grid small { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.5; }

  .closing-panel { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .closing-panel .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .closing-panel h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .closing-panel p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }
  .nav-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .divider { margin-block: var(--space-16) var(--space-8); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .case-toc ul { gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; }
    .case-toc li + li { margin-left: 0; }
    .case-toc li + li::before { content: none; }
    .agent-flow li { min-height: 150px; }
    .evolution-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .evolution-grid div { min-height: 0; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 130px; }
    .disclosure { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-meta { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc { margin-top: var(--space-6); margin-bottom: var(--space-12); padding-block: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6); }
    .case-toc a { min-height: 44px; }
    .workflow-compare { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .workflow-shift { transform: rotate(90deg); }
    .message, .report-preview { max-width: 100%; }
    .agent-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .agent-flow li { min-height: auto; }
    .nav-row .btn { width: 100%; text-align: center; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .outcome:hover,
    .case-meta div:hover,
    .workflow-panel:hover,
    .message:hover,
    .report-preview:hover,
    .agent-flow li:hover,
    .evolution-grid div:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
